---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ToolPanel from '../../components/ToolPanel.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Repair a corrupt FIT file, free and in your browser"
  description="Fix a Garmin or Wahoo FIT file that was cut off or will not upload. Recovers the readable records, rebuilds the summary and checksum. The file never leaves your device."
  schema={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'FIT file repair',
    applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any (web browser)',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  }}
>
  <h1>Repair a FIT file</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    For recordings that stopped mid-save, show as zero bytes of data, or are refused on upload. Choose the file and
    you will see what was wrong, what was recovered, and a repaired copy to download.
  </p>

  <ToolPanel tool="repair" />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What it fixes</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>What the tool does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>The recording was never finalised: the battery died, the device froze or restarted.</td>
          <td>
            Reads every complete record up to the cut, then rebuilds the lap, session and activity summary a platform
            expects from those records.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The header gives the wrong data size, often zero.</td>
          <td>Measures the real data and writes the correct size.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The checksum at the end is missing or wrong.</td>
          <td>Recalculates it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A block in the middle of the file is unreadable: overwritten, blank, or filled with leftover data.</td>
          <td>
            Skips the damaged bytes and picks the recording up where it becomes readable again, so the part after
            the damage is kept too.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The end of the file is blank padding or leftover data from the device's storage.</td>
          <td>Stops at the last real record and leaves the rest out.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Stray bytes sit in front of the file header.</td>
          <td>Finds the real start of the file.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What it cannot fix</h2>
    <p>
      It cannot bring back time the device never wrote to storage. Records inside a damaged block are gone; the tool
      tells you how many bytes it had to skip. Values that were overwritten with other plausible-looking numbers,
      rather than with garbage, cannot be told apart from real data, so a repaired file is worth a glance before you
      upload it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rebuilt summary covers distance, elapsed and moving time, average and maximum heart rate, average cadence
      and power, and speed. Calories and total ascent are left blank instead of being guessed; most platforms work
      those out again from the records.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <h3>It says my file is not damaged, but the upload still fails.</h3>
    <p>
      Then the cause is something else. The most common is that the platform already has that activity and treats
      the upload as a duplicate. Another is a file that is valid but is not an activity, such as a course or a
      settings file.
    </p>
    <h3>Why does it ask for the activity type?</h3>
    <p>
      Many devices store the sport only in the summary written when you press save. If that part was lost, the tool
      has no way to tell a run from a ride, so it asks. If the sport survived, it is used without asking.
    </p>
    <h3>Is the original changed?</h3>
    <p>No. The page can only read the file you pick. The repaired copy is a new download with <code>-repaired</code> in its name.</p>
    <p>
      For background on how these files break, read <a href="/guides/why-fit-files-get-corrupted/">why FIT files get corrupted</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
